// THE STORY OF ATLANTIS: A GEOGRAPHICAL, HISTORICAL, AND ETHNOLOGICAL SKETCH,
// BY W. SCOTT-ELLIOT, WITH A PREFACE BY A. P. SINNETT (LONDON: THEOSOPHICAL
// PUBLISHING SOCIETY, 1896).
//
// The text comes from scott-elliot-text.ts, generated by
// scripts/texts/atlantis-authors.py from Project Gutenberg #21796 (a later
// printing), voted word by word against the archive.org OCRs of three copies
// of the 1896 first edition (UCLA, and two Digital Library of India copies)
// and of the revised edition of 1909. The majority reading is taken; every
// reading the vote took and every correction made by hand is listed in the
// script, and the doubtful places were read on the UCLA page images. The whole
// book is imported: Sinnett's Preface and the text, which has no chapters,
// divided by the archive into 23 sections at the book's own heads, under a
// record for the book. The four maps are listed with the book.
//
// THE CLAIM. The Preface says the book's facts were gathered by "astral
// clairvoyance" from "the memory of Nature", with access to "maps and other
// records physically preserved"; the text says they were "drawn ... from
// contemporary records". The archive records that as the book's claim and
// gives it no year. No such record has been produced; the four maps are the
// author's own of 1896. Its dates (the catastrophes of 800,000, 200,000 and
// 80,000 years ago, the sinking of "Poseidonis" in 9564 BC) are its claims,
// not evidence, and no geological or archaeological evidence supports them.
// Its races and sub-races, and its ranking of peoples as advanced or
// "degraded", are the Theosophical race theory of its day: the text's own.
//
// Nothing is marked verified: the text is a careful copy, not a reading
// checked page by page against the book.

